-- title:
--   Equal-degree linear systems: heights agree up to ε h + C
-- statement:
--   Fix $N \ge 1$ and work with the field $\bar F_N :=$ [`ModularCurve.modularFunctionFieldBar N`](def/ModularCurve_ArithmeticGalois.html#L111), the subfield of $\overline{\mathbb Q}((q))$ generated over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ by the image of the $\mathbb Q$-subfield of $\mathbb Q((q))$ generated by the divisor expansions attached to $N$, viewed as an extension of $K := \overline{\mathbb Q} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`. Let $a, b \in \mathbb N$ and let $s : \mathrm{Fin}\,a \to \bar F_N$ and $u : \mathrm{Fin}\,b \to \bar F_N$ be finite families, all of whose members are nonzero. Let $D_1, D_2$ be divisors on $\bar F_N/K$, i.e. finitely supported $\mathbb Z$-valued functions on the places of $\bar F_N/K$ (valuation subrings containing $K$, proper and principal), with degree the sum of the coefficients weighted by the residue degrees. Assume $2g + 1 \le \deg D_1$, where $g =$ `genusFF` is the $K$-dimension of $H^1$ of the zero divisor, and $\deg D_1 = \deg D_2$. Assume further that the $K$-span of the range of $s$ is the Riemann–Roch space $\{f : v(f) \le \exp(D_1 v)$ for all places $v\}$, and that the $K$-span of the range of $u$ is the Riemann–Roch space of $D_2$. The conclusion: for every real $\varepsilon > 0$ there is a constant $C \in \mathbb R$ such that for every place $v$ of $\bar F_N/K$, $$\bigl| \mathrm{pointHt}(s, v) - \mathrm{pointHt}(u, v) \bigr| \le \varepsilon \cdot \mathrm{pointHt}(s, v) + C,$$ where [`AlgebraicCurve.pointHt`](def/ModularCurve_JZeroHeightForm.html#L41) of a family at $v$ is the absolute logarithmic height (normalised by the degree over $\mathbb Q$ of the field generated by the coordinates) of the vector obtained by evaluating at $v$ the quotients of the members of the family by a pivot member.
--
--   This is the degree-zero case of Weil's height machine for the curve $X_0(N)$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, in the elementary $\varepsilon$-form that compares the naive heights attached to two complete linear systems of the same degree without invoking canonical heights. It underlies the later height estimates on $J_0(N)$, among them [`ModularCurve.JZero.exists_absLogHeight_regVal_sub_two_mul_pointHt_le`](thm.html#ModularCurve.JZero.exists_absLogHeight_regVal_sub_two_mul_pointHt_le), [`ModularCurve.JZero.exists_abs_pairHt_sub_pointHt_div_le`](thm.html#ModularCurve.JZero.exists_abs_pairHt_sub_pointHt_div_le) and [`ModularCurve.JZero.exists_abv_evalAt_eq_abv_evalAt_of_le_prox`](thm.html#ModularCurve.JZero.exists_abv_evalAt_eq_abv_evalAt_of_le_prox).
